{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": ["ES2020", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["web-bluetooth", "w3c-web-serial", "vite/client"],

    /* Bundler mode */
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    /* Linting */
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,

    /* Sharing with the desktop build (../src): "@shared/*" is the code both
       targets keep byte-identical (../shared); "~/*" is this target's own src
       root, used only so shared code can reach a per-target seam file (see
       shared/hooks/useDiag.ts importing "~/link") without a relative path that
       would resolve outside shared/.

       The entries after those two pin ../shared's own external imports to this
       project's node_modules, which is what the layout already implies:
       shared/ is type-checked against web/'s dependencies. They are needed
       because TypeScript resolves a bare specifier by walking up from the
       *importing file*, so ../shared/**'s `import "react"` looks in
       ../node_modules — the desktop app's install. That exists locally, which
       is why this only ever broke in CI: .github/workflows/pages.yml installs
       web/ alone, so tsc walked up to a node_modules that was not there and
       reported every shared/ file as untyped.

       Each target is the package tsc would have picked itself had shared/ sat
       under web/: @types/react rather than react (react ships no
       declarations), and the studio client's lib/ rather than its root (a
       paths mapping is a plain file lookup, so the package's "exports" map —
       which is the only thing pointing at lib/ — is not consulted).

       Type checking only: "noEmit" is on and Vite does its own resolution, so
       none of this reaches the bundle. Vite's matching concern is the
       "resolve.dedupe" in vite.config.ts. */
    "paths": {
      "@shared/*": ["../shared/*"],
      "~/*": ["./src/*"],
      "react": ["./node_modules/@types/react"],
      "react/*": ["./node_modules/@types/react/*"],
      "react-dom": ["./node_modules/@types/react-dom"],
      "react-dom/*": ["./node_modules/@types/react-dom/*"],
      "@zmkfirmware/zmk-studio-ts-client": [
        "./node_modules/@zmkfirmware/zmk-studio-ts-client/lib/index"
      ],
      "@zmkfirmware/zmk-studio-ts-client/*": [
        "./node_modules/@zmkfirmware/zmk-studio-ts-client/lib/*"
      ]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src", "../shared"],
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }]
}
